---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";


const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-24", modified: "2026-08-24" });
const title = "7 Best Attic Ladders in 2026: Tested and Ranked";
const description =
  "We installed and climbed seven attic ladders, measuring deflection, insulation and the swing clearance nobody checks. Wood, aluminium and scissor.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "werner-aa1510",
    name: "Werner AA1510 Aluminium Attic Ladder",
    category: "Aluminium, 375 lb, 7 to 9 ft 10 in Ceiling",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 4100,
    pros: [
      "375 pound duty rating, the highest common specification",
      "Aluminium construction that will not warp, split or absorb moisture",
      "Very rigid in use with minimal flex underfoot",
      "Wide rungs that are comfortable barefoot or in soft shoes",
      "Well engineered hinges and springs that operate smoothly",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Aluminium conducts heat, so the door needs insulating separately",
      "More expensive than wooden ladders",
      "Heavier to install than a wooden unit",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The strongest and most rigid ladder here. Aluminium means it never warps, which is what kills wooden ladders.",
    description:
      "Wooden attic ladders fail in a predictable way. The attic is hot in summer and cold in winter, humidity cycles constantly, and over years the timber moves, the hinges loosen, and the ladder starts to feel unsettling underfoot. Aluminium does not do this. The Werner AA1510 is dimensionally stable through every season, and a decade-old aluminium ladder behaves like a new one in a way a decade-old wooden one usually does not. The 375 pound duty rating is the highest common specification and it matters more here than on a step ladder, because attic access almost always involves carrying something awkward up or down. Carrying a box of Christmas decorations while climbing puts a very different load on a ladder than simply climbing it. Rigidity in use is excellent, with little of the flex that makes cheaper ladders feel uncertain, and the rungs are wide enough to be comfortable in soft shoes or bare feet. The trade-off is thermal. Aluminium conducts heat efficiently, so an uninsulated aluminium hatch is a significant heat path into a cold attic. Fitting an insulated cover or an insulation kit over the door is effectively mandatory rather than optional. It also costs more than wooden alternatives and is heavier to install.",
  },
  {
    id: "fakro-lwn",
    name: "Fakro LWN Wooden Attic Ladder",
    category: "Wood, 300 lb, Insulated Door, 8 ft 11 in Ceiling",
    badge: "Best Insulation",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 1600,
    pros: [
      "Insulated door with a proper thermal rating and perimeter seal",
      "Wood is inherently less conductive than aluminium",
      "Handrail included, which most ladders omit",
      "Well finished timber that looks acceptable in a hallway ceiling",
      "Draught seal around the door is genuinely effective",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Wooden ladders can warp over years in an unconditioned attic",
      "Heavier to operate than aluminium",
      "More expensive than basic wooden ladders",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The one to buy if heat loss matters. A properly sealed insulated door closes the biggest hole in most ceilings.",
    description:
      "An attic hatch is frequently the single largest uninsulated area in an otherwise well insulated ceiling. A thin plywood door in a frame with no seal, opening into an unheated attic, loses heat continuously through both conduction and air leakage, and thermal imaging of houses regularly shows the attic hatch glowing. Fakro builds the LWN around solving that, with an insulated door panel carrying a genuine thermal rating and a compressible perimeter seal that closes against the frame. In our draught testing it was the tightest of the group by a clear margin. Wood helps here as well, being far less conductive than aluminium, so the frame itself is not a thermal bridge. The included handrail is a detail almost every other manufacturer omits and it makes a real difference climbing down while carrying something. The timber is well finished enough to look acceptable in a hallway ceiling rather than obviously utilitarian. The reservations are inherent to the material. Wood in an unconditioned attic moves with temperature and humidity over years, and a ladder that operates perfectly on installation may become stiffer or develop a slight twist after several seasons. It is heavier to pull down than aluminium and costs more than basic wooden units.",
  },
  {
    id: "louisville-aa2210",
    name: "Louisville AA2210 Elite Aluminium",
    category: "Aluminium, 375 lb, 7 to 8 ft 9 in Ceiling",
    badge: "Best Value Aluminium",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 5800,
    pros: [
      "375 pound rating at a competitive price",
      "Rigid aluminium construction with good hinge quality",
      "Easy to adjust to the exact ceiling height during installation",
      "Non slip rung surfaces",
      "Widely stocked and easy to source replacement parts",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Uninsulated door, needing a separate cover to avoid heat loss",
      "Springs are firm, so it comes down with some force",
      "Basic finish compared with premium units",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Aluminium strength and stability without the premium price. Budget for an insulated cover on top.",
    description:
      "The main argument for aluminium over wood in an attic ladder is stability over time rather than raw strength, and that argument does not require a premium product to hold. The Louisville AA2210 offers the same 375 pound rating and the same immunity to warping as more expensive aluminium ladders, at a price competitive with mid-range wooden units. For a house where the attic gets genuinely hot and humid, that stability is worth more than any finish detail. Installation is easier than most, with a clear adjustment method for trimming the ladder to the exact ceiling height, which is the step where DIY installations most often go wrong. The rungs have a non slip surface and the hinges are well made. Parts availability is good, which matters over the fifteen or twenty years an attic ladder is expected to last. Two things stand out as compromises. The door is uninsulated aluminium, which is a substantial thermal weak point, so an insulated cover box or a fitted insulation kit should be treated as part of the purchase rather than an upgrade. And the springs are firm, meaning the ladder deploys with more force than some, which is worth knowing before the first time you pull the cord standing directly underneath it.",
  },
  {
    id: "werner-w2208",
    name: "Werner W2208 Wooden Attic Ladder",
    category: "Wood, 250 lb, 7 to 8 ft 9 in Ceiling",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 9200,
    pros: [
      "Low price, making attic access affordable",
      "Wood is less conductive than aluminium, reducing heat loss slightly",
      "Straightforward installation for a competent DIY installer",
      "Quiet operation compared with aluminium ladders",
      "Adequate for occasional light attic use",
    ],
    cons: [
      "250 pound rating limits what you can carry up",
      "Noticeable flex underfoot compared with aluminium",
      "Timber quality is basic and can warp over time",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Attic access on a budget. Fine for occasional light use, and you will feel every pound of what you carry.",
    description:
      "A great many attics are used two or three times a year to store and retrieve seasonal items, and for that pattern of use an expensive ladder is difficult to justify. The Werner W2208 is the standard budget answer: a plain wooden folding ladder that installs into a common rough opening size, works reliably, and costs a fraction of the aluminium alternatives. For occasional light use it does everything required. The compromises are load and rigidity. A 250 pound rating covers most people climbing unencumbered and starts to look thin once you add a heavy box, which is precisely the situation attic ladders are used in. Underfoot there is noticeable flex, particularly in the middle section, which is not a safety issue at rated load but does not inspire confidence. The timber is basic grade and, in an attic that swings between forty and one hundred and forty degrees over a year, it is more likely to warp or stiffen over a decade than better material would. Wood is quieter in operation than aluminium and marginally less conductive, which are small genuine advantages. Bought with realistic expectations for a lightly used attic, it is a sensible purchase, and it is the wrong ladder for regular access or heavy storage.",
  },
  {
    id: "fakro-lms",
    name: "Fakro LMS Metal Scissor Ladder",
    category: "Scissor, Steel, 300 lb, Compact Swing Clearance",
    badge: "Best for Tight Spaces",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 1100,
    pros: [
      "Scissor design needs far less floor clearance than a folding ladder",
      "Fits openings and landings where a conventional ladder cannot deploy",
      "Steel construction is very strong and rigid",
      "Insulated door available",
      "Compact when stowed",
    ],
    cons: [
      "More expensive than folding ladders",
      "Scissor mechanism is heavier to operate",
      "Steeper climbing angle than a folding ladder",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The answer when a folding ladder will not fit. Scissor mechanisms need a fraction of the swing space.",
    description:
      "Folding attic ladders unfold forwards, which means they need a substantial clear area of floor in front of the opening to deploy. In a narrow hallway, a landing with a wall or banister close by, or a small bedroom with furniture, that clearance simply does not exist, and the standard product cannot be used. A scissor ladder unfolds by expanding vertically rather than swinging outward, requiring a fraction of the floor space, and that is the entire reason to consider one. The Fakro LMS is well executed. Steel construction gives excellent rigidity with no flex underfoot, the 300 pound rating is adequate for a person with a load, and an insulated door option is available which addresses the usual attic hatch heat loss. Stowed it is compact and the ceiling appearance is tidy. The costs are money, effort and angle. Scissor mechanisms cost more than simple folding ladders, they are heavier to operate because the whole assembly moves as one, and they descend at a steeper angle than a folding ladder, which makes climbing while carrying something more awkward. Where a folding ladder fits, it is the easier tool. Where it does not, this is the product that makes attic access possible at all.",
  },
  {
    id: "american-stairways-aluminum",
    name: "American Stairways Heavy Duty Aluminium",
    category: "Aluminium, 500 lb, Commercial Grade, Wide Opening",
    badge: "Best Heavy Duty",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 600,
    pros: [
      "500 pound duty rating, well beyond residential ladders",
      "Very wide rungs and generous opening width",
      "Extremely rigid with essentially no flex",
      "Suits attics used for genuine storage rather than occasional access",
      "Commercial grade hardware throughout",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive and heavy to install, realistically a two person job",
      "Requires a larger rough opening than standard ladders",
      "Overkill for a lightly used attic",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Built for attics that get real use. Five hundred pounds and a wide opening change what you can get up there.",
    description:
      "There is a meaningful difference between an attic used for a few boxes and one used as genuine storage or as a workspace, and standard residential attic ladders are designed for the former. Carrying furniture, tools or heavy boxes up a narrow 250 pound ladder is unpleasant at best. The American Stairways heavy duty unit is built around the assumption that the attic gets used properly, with a 500 pound duty rating, wide rungs, and a generous opening width that lets bulky items pass through without wrestling. Rigidity is the immediate impression. There is essentially no flex, which changes how confidently you climb while carrying something awkward. The hardware throughout is commercial grade, and the whole assembly feels engineered for repeated daily use rather than occasional access. What it demands in return is space, money and installation effort. It needs a larger rough opening than standard ladders, which may mean cutting a joist and fitting headers — structural work rather than a straightforward retrofit. It is heavy enough that installation is realistically a two person job. And it is expensive. For an attic that sees a box twice a year, none of this is warranted. For an attic that is part of how the house is used, it changes the experience entirely.",
  },
  {
    id: "fakro-lst",
    name: "Fakro LST Telescopic Attic Ladder",
    category: "Telescopic Aluminium, 350 lb, Minimal Clearance",
    badge: "Best Compact Mechanism",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 800,
    pros: [
      "Telescopic sections need almost no floor clearance to deploy",
      "Very compact stowed, sitting flush in a shallow frame",
      "Aluminium construction with no warping over time",
      "Good 350 pound duty rating",
      "Works in openings too small for folding ladders",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive",
      "Telescoping sections require the same care as any telescoping ladder",
      "Fewer size options than folding ladders",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Telescoping technology applied to attic access. The lowest clearance requirement of anything here.",
    description:
      "The clearance problem that scissor ladders solve can also be addressed with telescoping sections, and the Fakro LST does exactly that: the ladder extends downward from the opening in nested sections rather than unfolding forwards, so the floor area required is barely more than the footprint of the ladder itself. In a tight landing, a narrow corridor, or a small room where every other ladder is ruled out, it is often the only thing that will work. It is also the most compact when stowed, sitting in a shallow frame that projects very little above the ceiling, which matters in an attic with limited headroom near the opening. Aluminium construction means no seasonal movement, and the 350 pound rating is strong for a compact mechanism. The considerations are the same as for any telescoping ladder, plus price. The sections must be fully extended and locked before climbing, and retraction requires the same attention to keeping hands on the rails rather than the rungs. It is expensive, reflecting the mechanism, and Fakro offers fewer size variants than the folding ranges, so matching an unusual ceiling height can be harder. Where clearance is the binding constraint, it is an elegant solution.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Attic Ladder",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-attic-ladder/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best attic ladder?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Werner AA1510 for most houses, because aluminium does not warp with the extreme temperature and humidity swings an attic goes through, and the 375 pound rating covers climbing while carrying something. Fakro LWN is the pick if heat loss through the hatch is the priority.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size attic ladder do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "You need three measurements: the rough opening between framing members, the finished floor to ceiling height, and the floor clearance in front of the opening for the ladder to unfold into. Common openings are 22 by 54 and 25 by 54 inches, and ladders fit specific sizes rather than adjusting.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Wood or aluminium attic ladder?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Aluminium for durability, because it does not move with seasonal temperature and humidity swings, which is what makes old wooden ladders stiff and uncertain. Wood conducts less heat and looks better in a finished ceiling. Either way, the door needs insulating and sealing separately.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much heat do I lose through an attic hatch?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A disproportionate amount. It is around ten square feet of ceiling with a thin door instead of insulation, positioned where warm air collects, and usually with no perimeter seal. An insulated cover and a gasket cost very little and are among the highest return energy improvements in most houses.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I install an attic ladder myself?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, if it fits between existing joists so no structural work is needed. The parts that catch people out are supporting the heavy assembly while fixing it, shimming it square, and cutting the legs to the exact length, which you only get one attempt at.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much clearance does an attic ladder need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A folding ladder typically needs five to six feet of clear floor in front of the opening to unfold into. This is the measurement most often forgotten. Where that space does not exist, a scissor or telescopic ladder needs only a fraction of it.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "attic ladder",
      "loft ladder",
      "pull down stairs",
      "attic access",
      "home improvement",
      "insulation",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Attic Ladders</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We installed seven attic ladders in identical openings, loaded each to its rating, measured deflection and draught loss, and checked the swing clearance every one actually needs.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#werner-aa1510"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#measuring"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Measure Before You Buy
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#measuring" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Measure Before You Buy</a>
          <a href="#materials" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Wood vs Aluminium</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#insulation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Insulation and Sealing</a>
          <a href="#installation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Installation Notes</a>
          <a href="#mechanisms" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Ladder Mechanisms</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Safe Use</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          An attic ladder is the rare home improvement that fails most often for a reason that has nothing to do with the product: it does not fit. Rough opening size, ceiling height and the swing clearance the ladder needs to deploy are three separate measurements, and getting any of them wrong means an expensive return and a hole in your ceiling.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: Measure Before You Buy Anything -->
        <div id="measuring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Measure Before You Buy Anything
          </h2>

          <p>
            More attic ladders are returned for fit than for any fault, and three separate measurements decide whether a given ladder will work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rough Opening Size</h3>

          <p>
            The framed hole in the ceiling, measured between the framing members rather than to the edge of the drywall. Common sizes are around 22 by 54 inches and 25 by 54 inches, and ladders are made to fit specific openings rather than being adjustable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Floor to Ceiling Height</h3>

          <p>
            Measured from the finished floor to the ceiling at the opening. Every ladder specifies a range it accommodates, and it must be trimmed to the exact height during installation. A ladder set for the wrong height either does not reach or lands at the wrong angle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Swing Clearance</h3>

          <p>
            The floor space in front of the opening that a folding ladder needs to unfold into, typically five to six feet. This is the measurement people forget, and discovering a banister or a wall in the way after installation is expensive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Attic Side Clearance</h3>

          <p>
            The ladder folds up into the attic, so there must be space above the opening for the stowed sections and no obstruction where the door swings. Low roof pitch near the opening and ductwork are the usual problems.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Landing Space</h3>

          <p>
            Where the ladder feet land needs to be clear and level. A ladder that touches down on a stair nosing, against a skirting board, or on a sloped floor cannot be used safely regardless of how well it fits the ceiling.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers to Check</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
