        <!-- SECTION 2: Wood, Aluminium and Steel -->
        <div id="materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Wood, Aluminium and Steel
          </h2>

          <p>
            Material determines durability, weight, thermal performance and price, and the differences are more consequential in an attic than almost anywhere else in a house.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Aluminium does not move with the seasons.</strong> An attic swings through an enormous temperature and humidity range every year, and aluminium ignores it. This is the single strongest argument for the material, because seasonal movement is what makes old wooden ladders stiff and uncertain.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wood is less conductive.</strong> An aluminium frame is a direct thermal path between a heated hallway and a cold attic. Wood conducts far less, which makes a wooden ladder marginally better thermally even before considering the door.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Steel is the strongest and heaviest.</strong> Used mostly in scissor mechanisms, steel gives excellent rigidity and high duty ratings at the cost of weight, which is felt every time the ladder is operated.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Aluminium is lighter to operate.</strong> Pulling a ladder down and pushing it back up happens every time the attic is used, and an aluminium ladder is noticeably easier than a wooden one of the same size.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wood looks better in a finished ceiling.</strong> In a hallway with visible trim, a wooden frame can be painted or stained to match. Aluminium always looks like what it is, which matters in some houses and not at all in others.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Insulation is separate from material.</strong> Whichever you choose, the door is the thermal weak point and needs to be insulated and sealed. A good insulated wooden door beats a bare aluminium one, but an insulated aluminium door closes the gap entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

