            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best attic ladder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Werner AA1510 for most houses, because aluminium does not warp with the extreme temperature and humidity swings an attic goes through, and the 375 pound rating covers climbing while carrying something. Fakro LWN is the pick if heat loss through the hatch is the priority.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size attic ladder do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You need three measurements: the rough opening between framing members, the finished floor to ceiling height, and the floor clearance in front of the opening for the ladder to unfold into. Common openings are 22 by 54 and 25 by 54 inches, and ladders fit specific sizes rather than adjusting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Wood or aluminium attic ladder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aluminium for durability, because it does not move with seasonal temperature and humidity swings, which is what makes old wooden ladders stiff and uncertain. Wood conducts less heat and looks better in a finished ceiling. Either way, the door needs insulating and sealing separately.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much heat do I lose through an attic hatch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A disproportionate amount. It is around ten square feet of ceiling with a thin door instead of insulation, positioned where warm air collects, and usually with no perimeter seal. An insulated cover and a gasket cost very little and are among the highest return energy improvements in most houses.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install an attic ladder myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if it fits between existing joists so no structural work is needed. The parts that catch people out are supporting the heavy assembly while fixing it, shimming it square, and cutting the legs to the exact length, which you only get one attempt at.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much clearance does an attic ladder need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A folding ladder typically needs five to six feet of clear floor in front of the opening to unfold into. This is the measurement most often forgotten. Where that space does not exist, a scissor or telescopic ladder needs only a fraction of it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Attic Ladder Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Attic ladder problems divide neatly into things measured wrong before buying and habits formed after installing.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Swing Clearance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A folding ladder needs five to six feet of clear floor in front of the opening to unfold into, and a banister, a wall or a doorway in that space makes the ladder unusable. This is measured after the ceiling opening, not before, and discovering the problem post-installation is expensive.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting the Legs Short</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The final installation step trims the bottom section so the ladder lands flat at the correct angle, and there is no way to undo it. Measure with the ladder deployed and resting where it will sit, cut both legs identically, and check twice. A short ladder lands at the wrong angle permanently.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Door Uninsulated</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An attic hatch is around ten square feet of ceiling with no insulation, in the place warm air collects, usually with no perimeter seal. Fitting rigid foam to the attic side of the door and a compressible gasket around the frame costs very little and takes an hour.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Climbing With a Box in Your Arms</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Carrying a load up an attic ladder occupies both hands and hides the rungs from view, which is exactly how people fall. Haul items up on a rope, hand them to someone at the top, or make more trips. The convenience saved is not worth the risk taken.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

